::U2L1Q01::Which is health-related fitness? {=Cardiorespiratory endurance ~Agility ~Reaction time ~Speed}

::U2L1Q02::Which is skill-related fitness? {=Coordination ~Body composition ~Flexibility ~Muscular endurance}

::U2L1Q03::Muscular strength is the ability to do what? {=Exert force ~Sustain oxygen-supported activity ~Change direction ~Respond to a cue}

::U2L1Q04::Muscular endurance is the ability to do what? {=Repeat or sustain muscular effort ~Produce one rapid force ~Maintain tissue proportions ~Move quickly}

::U2L1Q05::Flexibility describes what? {=Available joint range of motion ~Reaction speed ~Cardiorespiratory support ~Direction change}

::U2L1Q06::Balance is the ability to do what? {=Maintain stability and body control ~Produce force rapidly ~Exercise for a deadline ~Measure tissue proportions}

::U2L1Q07::Agility is the ability to do what? {=Change direction efficiently under control ~Maintain sustained effort ~Exert maximal force ~Stretch every joint}

::U2L1Q08::Power is best defined as what? {=Producing force rapidly ~Repeated low-force effort ~Stable posture ~Sustained oxygen use}

::U2L1Q09::Reaction time measures what? {=How quickly a person responds to a stimulus ~How long activity lasts ~How far a joint moves ~How much force repeats}

::U2L1Q10::Speed is the ability to do what? {=Move quickly ~Maintain balance ~Coordinate body parts ~Sustain effort}

::U2L1Q11::Coordination is the ability to do what? {=Use body parts together efficiently ~Change tissue proportions ~Hold force over time ~Respond before a cue}

::U2L1Q12::Which neutral statement about body composition is correct? {=It refers to relative proportions of body tissues ~It proves personal worth ~Appearance accurately measures it ~It must be graded by body size}

::U2L1Q13::Physical activity differs from exercise because exercise is usually what? {=Planned activity intended to develop fitness ~Any body movement ~Always competitive ~Always intense}

::U2L1Q14::A steady 20-minute walk primarily involves which component? {=Cardiorespiratory endurance ~Power ~Reaction time ~Agility}

::U2L1Q15::Repeated controlled low-resistance movements emphasize what? {=Muscular endurance ~Muscular strength only ~Speed ~Reaction time}

::U2L1Q16::A brief rapid controlled jump most directly emphasizes what? {=Power ~Flexibility ~Body composition ~Cardiorespiratory endurance}

::U2L1Q17::Quickly responding to a visual signal emphasizes what? {=Reaction time ~Muscular endurance ~Flexibility ~Body composition}

::U2L1Q18::Safely changing direction around markers emphasizes what? {=Agility ~Flexibility ~Body composition ~Muscular strength}

::U2L1Q19::Holding a controlled stable position primarily emphasizes what? {=Balance ~Speed ~Power ~Cardiorespiratory endurance}

::U2L1Q20::Why can one activity have several classifications? {=Most activities require multiple fitness capacities ~Every component is always equally primary ~Categories have no meaning ~Only sport activities use fitness}

::U2L1Q21::What makes a component primary? {=It best matches the dominant activity demand or goal ~It appears first alphabetically ~It is always health-related ~It is the student's strongest ability}

::U2L1Q22::A long brisk walk uses muscular endurance as what type of component? {=A supporting component ~The only component ~An unrelated component ~A body-composition measure}

::U2L1Q23::Which evidence best supports classifying cycling as cardiorespiratory endurance? {=The cycling is sustained at an appropriate steady effort ~The bicycle has two wheels ~The student likes cycling ~The route has turns}

::U2L1Q24::Which fitness category primarily supports health, function, and lifetime capacity? {=Health-related fitness ~Skill-related fitness only ~Performance ranking ~Sport specialization}

::U2L1Q25::Which fitness category primarily supports movement efficiency and performance? {=Skill-related fitness ~Body composition only ~Health status ~Medical diagnosis}

::U2L1Q26::Which is the best evidence-based classification? {=A repeated task emphasizes endurance because the muscles work many times ~A familiar task develops everything equally ~Appearance shows strength ~Enjoyment proves speed}

::U2L1Q27::Which activity most directly matches a flexibility goal? {=A controlled joint-range routine ~A short response drill ~A sprint ~A direction-change drill}

::U2L1Q28::Which activity most directly matches a coordination goal? {=A controlled movement sequence using arms and legs together ~A static flexibility hold only ~A steady walk only ~A tissue measurement}

::U2L1Q29::How should students be graded in a fitness-component application? {=On analysis, evidence, and safe participation ~On being fastest ~On body size ~On outperforming classmates}

::U2L1Q30::What should a student do if an introductory activity becomes unsafe? {=Stop or modify and seek appropriate help when warranted ~Push through warning signs ~Increase intensity ~Hide the problem}

